so we were hearing there uh from the  chancellor amongst other things that he  was comfortable with the level of tax  that he was paying on his national  insurance do you think he should be  no i don't because i don't think that  the tax burden at the moment is falling  fairly i don't think it is right that  the national insurance increase  hits people in work and hits businesses  employing new people and yet if you get  your income from stocks and shares and  dividends or a portfolio of buy-to-let  properties you're not paying a penny  more in tax i don't think that strikes  the right balance especially at a time  when people are facing a cost of living  crisis this winter with petrol prices  gas and electricity bills the weekly  food shop all going up and the  government's response is taking more  money from people in taxes but he also  went on to say that taxes are starting  to be cut use referring to universal  credit and the amount of money that is  going to be added to try and offset the  six billion pounds that the government  is saving from reducing the 20 you know  all of this i'm just explaining it  obviously for our viewers because of  them taking away the 20 pounds a week  for some families um and he said you  know what people are just going to be  better off  that's just not true and it's not what  the institute fiscal studies  agreed with what they said  well  six billion pounds is being taken off  universal credit and the government put  less than half of that back in yesterday  um six million families lose out from  the 20 pounds a week  only 1.9 million families benefit from  the changes announced yesterday so it is  clear that some of those people on the  most  modest and low incomes are losing out  significantly by the chancellor's  announcements yesterday but also this  national insurance rise which comes in  next year costing more than 10 billion  pounds on families and businesses  actually that's the wrong tax at the  wrong time when people are facing all of  these additional costs now worries about  interest rates going up as well and not  asking those with the broadest shoulders  to pay a penny more i just don't think  that is the right balance i also think  more could have been done in the budget  yesterday around the cost of living  gas and electricity um bills five  percent of those the money goes directly  uh to the treasury in a in tax  we could cut that tax from five percent  to zero percent for six months to help  people get through these winter months  and that could be paid for by the fact  that vat receipts over the last few  months have been four billion pounds  more than forecast that's how labour  would help people with the cost of  living crisis right now and the  chancellor seems to be living in tens of  billions of pounds  a billion pounds for six months but vat  recedes over the last six months because  prices have been so high have come in  four billion pounds more than forecast  so labour would have made different  choices in the budget yesterday  different choices to ease the cost of  living crisis different choices on taxes  but also different choices to get the  economy growing because by the end of  this forecast period the office of  budget responsibility say that the  economy is just going to grow by 1.3 a  year  and that is much lower than what we've  experienced in the last 10 years and  much much lower than in the 10 years  before that and the truth is it's really  hard to lift living standards and  improve public services if the economy  is not growing properly so you need a  proper plan for growth that's why  investing in the zero carbon economy of  the future our plans to buy make and  sell more in britain so more good  quality jobs are created on these shores  are all essential for lifting living  standards and sustainably increasing and  improving public services tax rates are  at the highest rate uh since the 1950s  you know he's been described the  chancellor president chancellor has been  described as more brown than thatcher  well i i don't think that a budget from  gordon brown and certainly a budget from  me would have ignored the cost of living  crisis that's i think the real  difference between labor and  conservative right now and the issue  about taxes yes taxes are at their  highest level for more than 70 years  and yet public services are not the best  we've seen them for 70 years and that  burden of tax isn't falling fairly  nothing more to tax the online giants  who have made huge profits this last  year whilst high street businesses and  small businesses struggle nothing to ask  those with the broader shoulders to  contribute more just putting the burden  on working people through national  insurance and the businesses that employ  them you said yesterday you know  carrying on with that theme that  conservatives are now the party of high  taxation quoting you are labour going to  become the party of low taxation  the issue is around who is taxed and  under this government the burden falls  time and time again on the same people  working people on average incomes and  that's true whether it's the national  insurance contribution the freeze and  the personal allowance which means that  income taxes going up or indeed hidden  in the budget yesterday 6.9 billion  pounds extra in council tax over the  next few years that's always hitting the  same people  labor believe that those with the  broadest shoulders who get their incomes  through stocks and shares and buy to let  property portfolios and the online  giants should be paying a fairer share  of tax to ease the burden on ordinary  working people ahead of cop 26 the uh  chancellor said he is a green chancellor  i think the only way to be a responsible  chancellor today is to be a green  chancellor but look at what the  chancellor announced yesterday a cut in  air passenger duty for domestic flights  making it cheaper to fly from london to  manchester or from uh newcastle to  manchester those are the wrong decisions  and i find it astonishing the week  before cop 26 when we're supposed to be  showing global leadership that the  chancellor yesterday did nothing of the  sort and indeed with um tackling net  zero the chancellor is just putting the  burden on people  rather than taking a leadership role  himself so  there's huge opportunities with um with  electric vehicles with the hydrogen  industry with carbon capture and storage  to create good quality high skilled high  paid jobs in coastal communities in  former industrial communities and there  was almost no reference to that  yesterday in the chancellor's speech a  real missed opportunity to create those  high paid high skilled jobs of the  future in this country just before i let  you go tell me how the conversation went  yesterday morning hi rachel  um it's kia here  so um so at quarter to 12 45 minutes  before the budget began i was told  that kia had tested positive for covid  um i mean what a nightmare so i have 45  minutes basically to prepare my speech  now my background is as an economist and  i think i put that to good use  yesterday in getting on top of the  issues and and then sitting during the  budget listening to the chancellor's  speech and trying to respond to that uh  as quickly and as best as um i could so  i hope we did a good job yesterday  well it's the sort of thing you wake up  in a cold sweat in the middle of the  night it's  but luckily i didn't have uh  all that time to worry about it just had  to get on and do my job and how is he  he's good i spoke to him yesterday uh  after obviously frustrated um but as he  said to me earlier in the week i think  i'm the only person who hasn't got  covered  and  be careful what you say yeah good to  talk to you well done thank you thank

